-- title:
--   A double cone depends only on the line spanned by its axis
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$. Writing $V(v, \vartheta)$ for the double cone with axis $v$ and aperture $\vartheta$, one has
--   $$V(-v, \vartheta) \;=\; V(v, \vartheta).$$
--
--   Reversing the axis does not change the double cone: membership in $V(v,\vartheta)$ is a condition on the angle between the *line* spanned by the point and the line spanned by $v$, and that line is unchanged when $v$ is replaced by $-v$. This is the formal reason a double cone is an invariant of a line rather than of a direction, which in turn is why the reduction to finitely many reference cones works with a covering of projective space — antipodal pairs of directions — rather than of the sphere.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The statement is completely unconditional: no positivity or upper bound is imposed on $\vartheta$, $v$ need not be a unit vector, and the ambient space is an arbitrary real inner product space. In particular it also holds in the degenerate cases $v = 0$ and $\vartheta \notin [0, \pi]$.
